\section{Magnitude model}{

\code{magnitude_model = "c0eb"} (default) is an empirical-Bayes pooled conjugate
model, no Stan, 3 to 5 seconds for 24 donors. Per donor and visit it models the
any-positive fraction of the control, \code{q}, and the fold change \code{rho} of each
stimulation, both log-normal across donors. The across-donor means and
standard deviations are fitted from the data by marginal likelihood on a
grid, per parent population and stimulation, with a floor of 0.2 on the log
scale. The stimulated counts also inform \code{q}, so the baseline, which drives
the true RMS when responses are rare, is estimated from both samples. Within
the positive patterns each sample keeps C0's Dirichlet(\code{a0} + counts). Extra
arguments (\code{sd_min}, \code{pool_patterns}, \code{null_mass}) go in \code{fit_args}. A parent
population with fewer than 8 donor-visit blocks gets a warning: the
across-donor hyperparameters are then poorly determined (use \code{"c0"}).
Every control sample of a donor and visit measures the same baseline and
shares one \code{q} draw.

Because the fold change is pooled, a donor with no positive cell in either
sample gets an interval that reflects the cohort, not the donor. When the
cohort responds strongly the interval can lie wholly above 0 (for example
3e-5 to 3e-4 for a 10,000-cell sample), because the model says "if this donor
responds like the others, the effect is about this small". Its lower end stays
below the default \code{magnitude_floor} of 1e-4, so the donor is not
\code{magnitude_positive} and no \code{magnitude_without_response} flag is raised; with
\code{magnitude_floor = 0} it would be. A mixture with a point mass at no response
(\code{fit_args = list(null_mass = TRUE)}) did not change this when every donor
responds (the fitted mass was 0.001), and in dev simulations it cut false
flags at the cost of power (rare, weak: true positives 0.64 to 0.53, false
flags among non-responders 0.19 to 0.10, pooled Spearman -0.02), so it is off.

\code{"c0"} is the plain conjugate model: an independent Dirichlet(\code{a0} + counts)
per sample, no pooling. \code{"m1dm"} calls \code{cj_fit(model = "m1dm", level = "sample")}, which pools donors in Stan and needs CmdStan (14 to 24 seconds for
24 donors; arguments in \code{fit_args}, for example \code{level = "structured"}). A
\code{cj_fit} given as \code{x} is used as it is for the magnitudes, and
\code{magnitude_model} is ignored.

In the benchmark simulations (24 donors, 10,000 parent cells, 3 or 4 markers,
seeds 11 to 20, held out), Spearman with the true donor RMS, pooled over
scenarios and seeds, was:\tabular{lllll}{
   scenario \tab COMPASS FS \tab C0 \tab C0-EB (default) \tab m1dm \cr
   rare, weak \tab 0.50 \tab 0.69 \tab 0.82 \tab 0.82 \cr
   moderate \tab 0.72 \tab 0.98 \tab 0.99 \tab 0.99 \cr
}


Within one replicate at the rare, weak effect, the gain over FS was +0.06
(95\% interval -0.00 to 0.12 over seeds) for C0, +0.21 (0.15 to 0.27) for
C0-EB and +0.20 (0.15 to 0.26) for m1dm. The 90\% intervals covered the true
RMS in 0.85 to 0.94 of donor contrasts for C0-EB (0.89 to 0.94 at the rare,
weak effect), 0.92 to 0.96 for C0 and 0.91 to 0.98 for m1dm. The intervals
describe the sampled donor-visit: against a structured truth with
sample-level noise (generator B) they covered 0.43. \strong{CPS from C0 or C0-EB is
not reliable} (dev Spearman -0.6 to 0.7, coverage 0.2 to 1.00 across
scenarios): take CPS from \code{"m1dm"}. NICR and Poly-NICR bootstrap intervals
cover cell sampling within a sample only.
}

\section{Concordance}{

The COMPASS call is \code{compass_response_degree1 > response_threshold}
(\code{compass_call}). The magnitude is positive when the lower end of the RMS
interval exceeds \code{magnitude_floor} (\code{magnitude_positive}). \code{concordance} is
one of:
\itemize{
\item \code{"response_with_magnitude"}: call and positive magnitude;
\item \code{"no_response_no_magnitude"}: neither;
\item \code{"response_without_magnitude"}: COMPASS calls a response, the RMS interval
includes \code{magnitude_floor};
\item \code{"magnitude_without_response"}: no COMPASS call, the RMS interval lies
above \code{magnitude_floor};
\item \code{"not_assessed"}: the call or the magnitude is missing (for example a
contrast without a control sample, where no magnitude is defined, or a
failed COMPASS run).
}

\code{discordant} is \code{TRUE} for the two mismatch classes. A discordant row asks
for a look, not a verdict: COMPASS's call and a posterior interval answer
different questions.
}

